<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Compagnie des Îles de l'Amérique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1635–1651</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Company of the American Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Compagnie des Îles de l'Amérique was a French chartered company founded in 1635 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> to colonise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. It succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-de-saint-christophe">Compagnie de Saint-Christophe</a> and sponsored the settlement of Guadeloupe and Martinique in 1635. Unable to make a profit, it sold its islands in 1649–1651 to private proprietors, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, who bought Saint Christopher and neighbouring islands.</p></div>
